<p>Decimal 36879. Default value: 27. Also known as <strong>VICCRF</strong>.</p>
<p>Background colour, border colour and the inverse colour switch, packed into one
byte. In TRSE the easiest way to set it is to add one of the SCREEN_BG_ constants
(the background) to an ordinary colour constant (the border):</p>
<code>
// black screen with a cyan border
SCREEN_BG_COLOR := SCREEN_BG_BLACK + CYAN;
</code>
<p>The SCREEN_BG_ constants are SCREEN_BG_BLACK, WHITE, RED, CYAN, PURPLE, GREEN,
BLUE, YELLOW, ORANGE, LTORANGE, PINK, LTCYAN, LTPURPLE, LTGREEN, LTBLUE and
LTYELLOW. Each already has the inverse switch set to normal video.</p>

<p>The individual fields are:</p>

<p><strong>Bits 7-4:</strong> Default value: 1. The background colour of the screen. All 16
colours are available:</p>
<ul>
<li>Black - 0</li>
<li>White - 1</li>
<li>Red - 2</li>
<li>Cyan - 3</li>
<li>Purple - 4</li>
<li>Green - 5</li>
<li>Blue - 6</li>
<li>Yellow - 7</li>
<li>Orange - 8</li>
<li>Lt Orange - 9</li>
<li>Pink - 10</li>
<li>Lt Cyan - 11</li>
<li>Lt Purple - 12</li>
<li>Lt Green - 13</li>
<li>Lt Blue - 14</li>
<li>Lt Yellow - 15</li>
</ul>
<p>In multicolour mode this is the colour selected by the bit pair 00.</p>

<p><strong>Bit 3:</strong> Default value: 1. The inverse colour switch. When set to one the
background and foreground colours are in their usual places. Setting it to zero
inverts the scheme: the foreground colour is used for the background and the
characters are drawn in the background colour.</p>
<p>This bit has no effect on characters that are in multicolour mode.</p>

<p><strong>Bits 2-0:</strong> Default value: 3. The border colour. Only the first eight
colours can be used here:</p>
<ul>
<li>Black - 0</li>
<li>White - 1</li>
<li>Red - 2</li>
<li>Cyan - 3</li>
<li>Purple - 4</li>
<li>Green - 5</li>
<li>Blue - 6</li>
<li>Yellow - 7</li>
</ul>

<p>See also SCREEN_COL_LOC for the per character foreground colours, and
AUX_COLOR_AND_VOLUME ($900E) for the fourth multicolour colour.</p>
